  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nQuite a bit of mid-and-high-level cloud coverage for our \nHalloween this year with plenty of moisture aloft. Precipitable \nwater values should still be in the 1.0-1.2\" range, well above \nnormal for the end of October. Broad ridging flow aloft and the \nlack of surface instability due to cloud coverage will lead to a \n\"mostly\" dry forecast for Halloween night. Can't rule out a stray \nrain shower or two across Southwest New Mexico, but 95% of the \nregion will be dry tonight. Temperatures will peak in the upper \n60s/lower 70s this afternoon, only cooling a few degrees tonight \nwith the aforementioned clouds keeping us rather mild overnight.\nWind will be light and variable. I don't see anything that will \ninterrupt outdoor festivities tonight, so have fun everybody!\n\nTwo features to focus on for Sunday fixed across the Southwestern\nU.S. will be a strengthening subtropical jet streak reaching \n95-knots at 500mb and a diving Pacific low right behind it. This \nwill lead to yet another round of rain chances for the area, along\nwith a frontal boundary passage between the Sunday evening-early \nMonday morning timeframe. Scattered rain showers beginning along \nthe AZ/NM state line as early as Sunday morning will bring a risk \nof training storms and flash flooding through the day. Main risk \nwill be across Grant/Hidalgo Counties Sunday afternoon in time \nwith frontal passage. Enhanced lifting along the front will lead \nto more widespread showers and thunderstorms, with a storm motion \nparallel to the boundary due to the subtropical jet aloft posing a\nrisk of training storms and heavy rain \"streaks\". Most likely\nrainfall across Grant County will be 0.50-1.00, but a few spots \nof 1.50-2.00\" will be possible. A Flood Watch is in effect for \nmost of the Gila and upper Mimbres watersheds. Breezy southwest \nwinds will exist ahead of this Pacific front, generally 20-30 mph \nSunday afternoon across Southwest New Mexico.\n\nLess concerned about heavy rain for the Rio Grande valley and\nsouth-central New Mexico region as dry air entrainment at the \nbase of the Pacific low may lead to weaker and lower coverage rain\nshowers. Even less concerned further SE into Far West Texas, \nwhere rain chances are lower. Still, we can expect scattered rain \nshowers Sunday night-Monday morning for the I-25/US-54 corridors \nas the frontal boundary moves through.\n\nFull-on dry-slotting on Monday behind the Pacific low. This will \nlead to a breezy and cool dry with mostly clear skies. West winds \n15 to 20 mph is the current forecast. A few lingering rain showers\nmay exist over the Sacramento Mountains, but are not expected to\nlast through Monday morning.\n\nDare I say it, next week finally looks dry for New Mexico and \nwest Texas. This will be a much-needed reprieve from rain after a\nvery wet September and October. Lowland highs in the lower 70s \nwill be right near normal for early November. No major swings in \ntemperature expected and winds look generally light.",
